Cryptosporidiosis
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Profuse watery diarrhoea, Crampy abdominal pain, Exposure to recreational water, Modified acid-fast positive oocysts

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-004
Reason for Visit: Unremitting profuse watery diarrhoea leading to signs of acute dehydration requiring urgent fluid status assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated profuse, non-bloody watery diarrhoea over 10 times a day with an inability to maintain hydration, accompanied by crampy abdominal pain. Urgent clinical assessment is required to evaluate intravascular volume status and prevent severe hypovolaemia prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have pure liquid coming out of me constantly and my stomach is in knots."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced abruptly 5 days ago with profuse, watery, non-bloody diarrhoea. The patient reports severe, crampy, and intermittent abdominal pain, alongside a low-grade fever, significant nausea, recurrent vomiting, and profound anorexia. Patient notes an unintentional weight loss of approximately 3 kg secondary to fluid loss. History reveals recent exposure to a public municipal swimming pool 8 days prior.
Functional Status: Ambulatory but profoundly weak; restricted to the bedroom/bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, increasing the risk of unobserved collapse due to orthostatic hypotension.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 | HR: 115 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Pale, lethargic, dry mucous membranes, delayed capillary refill (3 seconds).
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, non-distended, with mild diffuse tenderness. Hyperactive bowel sounds. Poor skin turgor noted over the sternum. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Profuse watery diarrhoea, Crampy abdominal pain, Exposure to recreational water (Modified acid-fast positive oocysts pending lab confirmation).
Environmental Observations: Poor oral fluid intake visible; only plain tap water available at bedside.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cryptosporidiosis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Giardiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of foul-smelling, greasy, floating stools (steatorrhea) and a more acute, profuse presentation.

Viral Gastroenteritis (Norovirus) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prolonged duration (5 days) and the specific recreational water exposure history.

Cholera - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of relevant travel history to an endemic area, despite the profound watery nature of the stool.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with profound volume depletion secondary to a high-output diarrhoeal illness, evidenced by tachycardia, borderline hypotension, and delayed capillary refill. Given the continuous fluid losses and current inability to match output with oral intake, immediate clinical evaluation and aggressive oral rehydration strategy implementation were critical. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period posed a high risk of hypovolaemic shock and acute kidney injury, strictly satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe high-electrolyte oral rehydration salts (ORS). Request stool sample for modified acid-fast staining and multiplex PCR. Advise strict lactose avoidance.
Positive Behavioral Change: By setting a timer to consume 100mL of ORS every 30 minutes, the patient will actively restore their intravascular volume, which will reward them with an immediate reduction in lightheadedness and a return of physical strength, reinforcing the consistent hydration schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours for clinical review and stool results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if the patient faints upon standing, ceases to pass urine for more than 8 hours, develops severe localized abdominal pain, or experiences confusion.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending specialized stool microscopy; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The disease is typically self-limiting to 1-2 weeks in immunocompetent individuals. Diarrhoea will gradually taper in frequency.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if you feel like you are going to pass out when you stand up, or if your vomit contains blood or looks like coffee grounds.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Plain water will not replace the specific salts you are losing, which is causing your weakness. The ORS provides the exact ratio of sodium and glucose to force water back into your bloodstream.
Actionable Steps: Avoid dairy products entirely for the next week as your gut is temporarily unable to digest lactose. Sip ORS constantly.

SMS INFORMATION

Presumed Cryptosporidiosis (waterborne parasite). Strict adherence to electrolyte fluids required. Avoid all dairy products for 7 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Drink 100mL of electrolyte fluid every 30 mins. Go to ED immediately if you faint, stop passing urine entirely, or become confused.
